    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the technical details for your cedar deck in Vancouver.\n\nThe Vancouver building code specifies different load requirements for residential structures, including decks. For a single-story house with a cedar deck, you'll need to consider both the dead load and the live load.\n\n**Dead Load (DL):** This is the weight of the deck itself, including the decking material, joists, and any permanent fixtures. The dead load is typically calculated by multiplying the area of the deck by the weight per square foot of the decking material. For cedar, the weight per square foot can vary, but a common value is around 2 to 3 pounds per square foot (psf).\n\n**Live Load (LL):** This is the weight of temporary objects, such as people, furniture, and snow. The Vancouver building code often requires a live load of 40 psf for residential decks.\n\n**Total Load (TL):** The total load is the sum of the dead load and live load. For safety, you should design for the greater of the two loads, which in most cases will be the live load.\n\n**Joist Spacing:** The spacing of the joists will depend on the load they need to support and",
